# XSH seteuid()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Appropriate privileges are implementation-defined (XBD 3.21). Each test
# runs in a child of a privileged test process and first probes, in a
# disposable process and the setup phase, a call that only a privileged
# process may make (seteuid() to an ID the process does not hold); the
# test is UNRESOLVED when the probe does not show the state the assertion
# needs. User IDs used: 65534 and 65533 (given to the process), 65532
# (probes), 65531 (refused targets).

assertion  XSH.seteuid.001
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges and all user IDs 0, seteuid(65534)
           returns 0, geteuid() then returns 65534 and getuid() still 0.
needs      root
note       The saved set-user-ID equals the real user ID here, so whether
           it changed cannot be seen; XSH.seteuid.002 checks it.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/001-privileged.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.002
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real user ID
           65534 and effective and saved set-user-ID 65533: seteuid(65534)
           returns 0 with geteuid() and getuid() 65534, and seteuid(65533)
           (the saved set-user-ID, still 65533) then returns 0 with
           geteuid() 65533 and getuid() 65534.
needs      root
note       setreuid() (XSI) sets the IDs apart, so the test runs only on
           XSI systems; the requirement itself is not XSI.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/002-unprivileged.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.003
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real and saved
           set-user-ID 0 and effective user ID 65534 gets -1 with EPERM from
           seteuid(65531), and geteuid() is still 65534.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/003-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.004
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#ERRORS/EPERM
class      required
since      2001
says       The same process gets -1 with EPERM from seteuid(65534): its
           current effective user ID is neither its real user ID nor its
           saved set-user-ID, which are the only values allowed without
           privileges.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/004-eperm-effective.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.005
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.408
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges, seteuid((uid_t)-1) returns -1 with
           EINVAL ((uid_t)-1 is never a valid user ID) and geteuid() stays
           0.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/005-einval.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.006
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges and the supplementary group list set
           to {65534}, seteuid(65533) leaves the list getgroups() reports
           unchanged.
needs      root
note       The list is set with pc_drop_groups() (setgroups(), not in
           POSIX, where the system has it); without a supplementary group
           the test is UNRESOLVED.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/006-groups.c

assertion  XSH.seteuid.007
anchor     functions/seteuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9
class      required
since      2001
says       When one thread of a privileged process calls seteuid(65534),
           another thread of the process then gets 65534 from geteuid().
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/007-threads.c
